CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ class without operators: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ operator==: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ conversion: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
C's member-wise struct comparison made `a == b' valid for a class without
operator==, so `requires { a == b; }' held.  libc++'s equality_comparable,
and with it __cpp17_input_iterator, was then true for an iterator without
operator==, and iterator_traits took the wrong specialization.
